      "lineNbr": 69,
      "author": {
        "id": 1069543
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-14T17:26:26Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We generally do not take any UAPI changes that haven\u0027t landed upstream. See https://source.android.com/docs/core/architecture/kernel/kernel-code#core-kernel-features. This becomes unsupportable if a conflicting change is made upstream.\n\nIs this needed by system code, or are the consumers of this new UAPI restricted to vendor code?",
      "parentUuid": "f3992f6b_4b503c39",
      "revId": "1952cbeb1f61da8e5db3cff544dd35402ad4c57c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "42c256b7_dfc50eda",
        "filename": "include/uapi/linux/dma-heap.h",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 69,
      "author": {
        "id": 2055842
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-14T17:39:18Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e This becomes unsupportable if a conflicting change is made upstream.\n\nAgree. I don\u0027t actaully think that this particular implementation will be sent upstream, but there is a small possibility that some inner implementation (from some third-party) of dma restricted heaps will be merged to the linux mainline and will define the API for this, as said before.\n\n\u003e Is this needed by system code, or are the consumers of this new UAPI restricted to vendor code?\n\nI\u0027m pretty sure that it will be limited to vendor code, since we plan the restricted heap implementation to be a pure vendor module.",
      "parentUuid": "70ec00cb_94bce6f7",
      "revId": "1952cbeb1f61da8e5db3cff544dd35402ad4c57c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
